00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.248
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi.

00:00:01.248 --> 00:00:04.260
I'm Mike Coleman, a developer
advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.030
And in this video,
I want to show you

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.100
how you can use
Migrate for Anthos

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.340
to migrate IBM WebSphere
applications to containers

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.577
in just a few clicks.

00:00:13.577 --> 00:00:15.660
We'll start by looking at
the system requirements.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.100
Then I'll provide
a quick overview

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.510
of the migration process.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.060
Then we'll jump into
an actual migration.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.900
Now, this video is part of a
series on Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.940
So if you haven't checked
out those first videos

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.610
in the series, you might want
to go back and watch those,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.562
as this video builds on them.

00:00:31.562 --> 00:00:33.270
You can find the link
in the description.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.763
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.450
Before we get started, I wanted
to clarify that in this video,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.740
I'm discussing the migration
of traditional IBM WebSphere

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.310
Application Server and IBM
WebSphere Application Server

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.240
Network Deployment applications
rather than IBM WebSphere

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.320
Liberty apps.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.270
Keep that distinction
in mind when

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.570
you hear me say WebSphere
Application Server.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.450
Migrate for Anthos lets you
modernize app workloads running

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.580
in WebSphere Application
Servers by converting them

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.470
to application containers.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.330
You can then deploy
the app containers

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.810
to GKE or Anthos
clusters on Google Cloud,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.940
Anthos clusters on VMware,
and Anthos clusters on AWS.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.780
A WebSphere Application Server
VM can contain multiple apps.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.420
Migrate for Anthos helps you
automate the modernization

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.130
of WebSphere
Application Server apps

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.550
to containers by discovering
deployed apps in the source VM

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.130
and automatically
suggesting the configuration

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.020
for the modernization.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.330
With Migrate for Anthos,
you migrate each app

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.800
to its own container.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.650
This allows you to test and
deploy the migrated apps

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.470
individually rather than
having to test and deploy

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.360
multiple apps together.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.250
Regardless of whether
or not your source

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.670
apps were running WebSphere
Application Server or WebSphere

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.340
Application Server
Network Deployment,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.390
the resulting container image
will be the official IBM

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.530
WebSphere Application Server
traditional container image.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.530
And for those of you wondering,
the original responsibilities

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.500
of network deployment--
clustering, scheduling,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.870
version roll-outs, request
routing, et cetera--

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.390
will now be offloaded
to Kubernetes.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.760
Migrate for Anthos supports
the migration of apps

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.970
hosted on the following versions
of IBM WebSphere Application

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.170
Server--

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.820
WebSphere Application
Server traditional 8.5.5.x

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.650
and WebSphere Application
Server traditional 9.0.5.x.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.780
Migrate for Anthos
extracts two types

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.820
of configurations from the VM--

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.400
the configuration
for each application

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.930
and the configuration
of the target profile,

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.010
which defines the runtime
environment of a WebSphere

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.490
Application Server, including
ports, the Java virtual machine

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.470
settings, et cetera.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.810
The Migrate for Anthos
software automates the use

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.020
of the IBM WebSphere Application
Server Migration Toolkit

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.560
for application binaries to
discover, assess, and generate

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.890
migration reports, as well as
creating configuration scripts.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.270
Before you can
start a migration,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.030
you must upload the toolkit
to a Google Cloud Storage

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.650
bucket in your account.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.920
Check the documentation for more
information on this procedure.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.800
When migrating WebSphere
Application Server apps,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.560
the processing cluster can be
deployed on Google Kubernetes

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.780
Engine or an Anthos cluster on
Google Cloud, VMware, or AWS.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.820
Regardless of where
the cluster runs,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.770
it must use a Cloud
Storage bucket

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.790
as the repository for the
resulting migration artifacts.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.310
For more information on
creating a processing cluster,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.570
check out the first
video in this series.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.700
You can find a link in
the description below.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.570
And be sure to read the docs
to get the full details on all

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.760
the system requirements.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.480
With the prerequisites
out of the way,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:43.870
let's take a look at the
general migration flow.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.120
First, you're going to add
a migration source, which

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.070
represents the source
platform from which

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.510
you will be migrating.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.550
Then you're going to
create the migration.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.910
And that specifies the VM you're
actually going to migrate.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:56.910
You'll edit your
migration plan, including

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.660
selecting the WebSphere
Application Server

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.230
app that you want to migrate.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.980
And you should migrate one
WebSphere Application Server

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.545
app per migration.

00:04:04.545 --> 00:04:06.670
Then you're going to actually
perform the migration

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.650
to generate the app
container artifacts,

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.930
and then review those
artifacts in preparation

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.730
for building the
deployable app image.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.880
Now, use the migrated
artifacts to build

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.220
a container for
your migrated app,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.760
and then deploy your migrated
app to your target cluster.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.870
Finally, test the migrated app
and validate the migration.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.750
Note that if your source
VM has multiple apps,

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.580
you're going to need
to repeat steps 3

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.090
through 7 for each application
you wish to migrate.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.970
Also, you may have noticed if
you've watched previous videos,

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.660
there's not an explicit step
to assess the application using

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.710
the Discovery tool.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.540
That's because the
assessment happens

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.130
when you run the migration.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:46.630
All right.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.510
Let's go ahead and jump
into an actual migration.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.640
We're going to take a
source VM and extract

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.740
a single application
from that VM,

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.310
then deploy it to a GKE cluster
following the basic flow

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.330
that I just outlined.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.950
As you can see in
the Cloud Console,

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:05.310
I've actually already created
my migration processing cluster

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.690
and my migration source.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.650
I've also gone in and I've
stopped my WebSphere VM.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.180
With those things
out of the way,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.910
I'm all ready to start by
creating the actual migration.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.610
And to do that, I'll
move into the Cloud Shell

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.393
and issue the migctl
create command.

00:05:20.393 --> 00:05:22.560
Most of these parameters
are pretty straightforward,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.020
but I wanted to point out
this app-type property

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.020
at the bottom.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.230
That's where we specify that
we're actually migrating

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.060
a WebSphere application.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.350
Creating a migration is
going to take a few minutes,

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.058
so I'm going to pause
the video, and I'll

00:05:34.058 --> 00:05:35.220
come back when it's done.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.020
With the migration
assessment completed,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.150
I wanted to show you the
application discovery

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.530
report that was generated.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.730
It's stored up in a
Cloud Storage bucket.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.900
So let's navigate to the
actual report and load it up.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.215
Now, I'm not going to go
through the report in detail.

00:05:48.215 --> 00:05:50.340
I just want you to know
that it's available to you,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.340
and that it will give you
a bunch of information

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:54.510
on the applications
that were scanned.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.100
Let's move back
into Cloud Shell.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.920
And I need to download and
edit my migration.yaml.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.650
So I'll use migctl
get migration.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.170
And then I'm going to go
in and edit that file,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.510
and here are these
path variables.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.127
Now, they represent
the applications

00:06:08.127 --> 00:06:09.960
that were found during
the scanning process.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.160
And I only need one of them.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.930
You only migrate one
application at a time.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.940
So I'm going to delete this
query.ear path location here.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:19.950
And then the other
thing I want to do

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.560
is I want to go down
to the default app name

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.300
and change that from
WebSphere to something else.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.580
So I'll make that change here.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.860
And I'll actually
make the change

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.900
for the name of the Docker
image that's created as well.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.200
We use migctl to upload
our updated migration plan,

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.630
so that when we execute the
migration in the next step,

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.190
these new settings are used.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:41.830
And so let's go
ahead and do that.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.050
I'll clear the screen.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.280
And then I'm going to use migctl
to execute the migration plan

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.557
and generate my
migration artifacts.

00:06:48.557 --> 00:06:51.140
This will take a few minutes,
so I'm going to pause the video,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.540
and I'll rejoin when it's done.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.480
migctl is finished, so let's go
ahead and grab those artifacts

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.257
using migctl get-artifacts.

00:07:00.257 --> 00:07:01.840
I will list out the
directories so you

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:02.923
can see which was created.

00:07:02.923 --> 00:07:06.100
We've got a Docker file,
our application's .ear file,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.800
and this wsadmin.py file.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.080
This is where a bunch of
settings for your application

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:11.650
will be held.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:14.500
And you may need to edit that,
depending on your application.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.000
Finally, there's a
build script that

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.890
automates the creation
of our Docker image

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:20.560
and uploads it to the
Container Registry.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.020
I'll go ahead and
execute our build script.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.300
The build file takes
several minutes to run.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:27.520
I've sped up the video
so you can see what's

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.880
happening in the background.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.640
It's going to pull
the base Docker image.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.190
Then it runs through the
configuration of the WebSphere

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:35.470
server, including copying
in the application.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:38.650
And then finally, it pushes that
completed container image up

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.820
into the Registry.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.420
Now I'm going to go ahead and
edit the deployment.yaml file,

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:45.820
which by default provides
a headless cluster IP

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:49.060
service which is only accessible
from within the cluster.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.010
I'm going to change that
to type LoadBalancer

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:54.050
so we can access our
service externally.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:56.120
All that's left to do
now is to actually deploy

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.470
the application, and we'll do
that using kubectl apply -f

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.047
deployment_spec.yaml.

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:03.630
That's going to
deploy that deployment

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:04.832
spec we just looked at.

00:08:04.832 --> 00:08:06.290
So we're going to
create a service,

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:07.748
we're going to
create a deployment,

00:08:07.748 --> 00:08:10.010
and then we can check
the status of those

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:14.620
by using kubectl get pods
and kubectl get service.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.660
Now, from the service, we can
grab the external IP address

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.330
here and see that our
application, a movie

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:24.260
rental service, is up and
running in our browser.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.150
And that's all it
takes to migrate

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.550
a WebSphere application
running in a VM

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.270
to running on Kubernetes.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:32.240
There you have it.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.419
We've successfully
migrated an application

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:37.059
from WebSphere Application
Server running in a VM

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.220
to a container running on GKE.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:41.920
If you haven't already, be
sure to like and subscribe,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.420
as we'll be adding additional
videos to the series.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.480
And feel free to check
out Migrate for Anthos

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.270
up on Google Cloud today.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

